.TH CB 1 "August 2026" "cb 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cb \- copy text, file contents, or piped input to the clipboard
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cb
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILES...\fR | \fITEXT...\fR]
.br
.B echo
"hello" | cb
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cb
is a cross-platform clipboard utility that copies text, file contents, or piped
input into the system or terminal clipboard. It automatically selects an
appropriate clipboard backend based on the current environment, degrades
gracefully when no clipboard is available, and provides configurable defaults,
safe size limits, and colored log messages.

By default, \fBcb\fR:
.IP \[bu] 2
Copies plain text arguments directly to the clipboard.
.IP \[bu]
Copies the contents of files if file paths are passed.
.IP \[bu]
Copies piped input when used in a pipeline.
.IP \[bu]
Selects a clipboard backend automatically (see BACKENDS below).
.IP \[bu]
Limits maximum total copied size to 1MB for safety.

Configuration can be defined system-wide in
.IR /etc/cbrc
or per-user in
.IR ~/.cbrc ,
which is automatically created if missing.

.SH BACKENDS
.B cb
uses a clipboard backend resolver. Backends are optional runtime dependencies;
none are required globally. Automatic selection order:

.IP 1. 3
\fBpbcopy\fR — macOS native clipboard
.IP 2.
\fBwl-copy\fR — Wayland (\fIwl-clipboard\fR) when a Wayland session is active
.IP 3.
\fBxsel\fR — X11 when \fBDISPLAY\fR is set
.IP 4.
\fBxclip\fR — X11 when \fBDISPLAY\fR is set and \fBxsel\fR is unavailable
.IP 5.
\fBosc52\fR — terminal clipboard via OSC 52 (SSH, headless, or fallback)

Use \fB--show-backend\fR to see which backend would be selected.
Use \fB--backend=NAME\fR to force a specific backend.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -d ", " --dir
Include files from directories (non-recursive by default).
.TP
.BR -r ", " --recursive
Recursively include files in subdirectories (requires \fB-d\fR).
.TP
.BR -i ", " --include-dirs
Include directory names themselves as plain text.
.TP
.BR --max-size=\fIN\fR
Set the maximum total size allowed for all inputs. Units supported:
.B B, KB, MB, GB.
Default: \fB1MB\fR.
Use \fB--max-size=0\fR to disable safety checks.
.TP
.BR --backend=\fINAME\fR
Select clipboard backend:
.BR auto ", " pbcopy ", " wl-copy ", " xsel ", " xclip ", " osc52 .
Default: \fBauto\fR.
.TP
.B --show-backend
Print the resolved clipboard backend and exit. With \fB-v\fR, also prints the
selection reason and environment diagnostics.
.TP
.B --dry-run
Simulate the copy operation without modifying the clipboard. Reports payload
size and resolved backend in verbose mode.
.TP
.B --show-config
Display effective configuration (after merging system, user, and CLI overrides).
.TP
.BR -v ", " --verbose
Enable debug messages including OS, session type, display variables, and
selected backend.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Show a detailed help menu.

.SH CONFIGURATION
The following files are read, in order:
.IP \[bu] 2
\fI/etc/cbrc\fR (system-wide defaults)
.IP \[bu]
\fI~/.cbrc\fR (user-specific, silently created if missing)

The following variables can be set in configuration:
.IP \[bu] 2
\fBMODE\fR — clipboard mode (reserved for future use).
.IP \[bu]
\fBDIR\fR — include directory contents by default (true/false).
.IP \[bu]
\fBRECURSIVE\fR — enable recursive scanning (true/false).
.IP \[bu]
\fBINCLUDE_DIRS\fR — include directory names as text (true/false).
.IP \[bu]
\fBMAX_SIZE\fR — maximum allowed size (e.g., "2MB").
.IP \[bu]
\fBBACKEND\fR — default backend (auto, pbcopy, wl-copy, xsel, xclip, osc52).
.IP \[bu]
\fBOSC52_MAX_SIZE\fR — maximum OSC 52 payload in bytes (default: 1MB).

Configuration files are shell-sourced. Treat them like shell scripts and
restrict write permissions appropriately.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DISPLAY
X11 display; required for \fBxsel\fR/\fBxclip\fR backends.
.TP
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
Wayland display; used with \fBwl-copy\fR backend detection.
.TP
.B XDG_SESSION_TYPE
Session type; \fBwayland\fR enables Wayland backend detection.
.TP
.B SSH_CONNECTION
Indicates an SSH session; influences OSC 52 fallback messaging.
.TP
.B TERM
Terminal type; must not be \fBdumb\fR for OSC 52.
.TP
.B TMUX
When set, OSC 52 sequences are wrapped for tmux passthrough.
.TP
.B STY
When set, OSC 52 sequences are wrapped for GNU screen passthrough.

.SH COMPATIBILITY MATRIX
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
Environment@Backend
macOS Terminal/iTerm/etc.@pbcopy
Linux Wayland@wl-copy
Linux X11@xsel / xclip
FreeBSD X11@xsel / xclip
SSH/headless@osc52
tmux over SSH@osc52 (passthrough)
GNU screen over SSH@osc52 (passthrough)
non-interactive CI@unsupported unless explicitly configured
.TE

.SH SSH AND OSC 52
On headless servers or over SSH where no native graphical clipboard exists,
\fBcb\fR can copy to the \fIlocal terminal clipboard\fR using the OSC 52 escape
sequence. This requires:

.IP \[bu] 2
An interactive terminal (controlling TTY available)
.IP \[bu]
A terminal emulator that supports OSC 52 clipboard sync
.IP \[bu]
Optional tmux/screen passthrough configuration for nested sessions

OSC 52 payload size is limited (default 1MB, configurable via
\fBOSC52_MAX_SIZE\fR). Many terminals enforce their own limits.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Copy plain text:
.B cb "Hello World"
.TP
Copy contents of a file:
.B cb file.txt
.TP
Copy all top-level files in a directory:
.B cb -d ./docs
.TP
Recursively copy all files up to 5MB:
.B cb -d -r ./src --max-size=5MB
.TP
Copy piped input:
.B echo "test" | cb
.TP
Show resolved backend:
.B cb --show-backend
.TP
Force OSC 52 over SSH:
.B cb --backend=osc52 "deploy logs"
.TP
Dry run:
.B cb --dry-run file.txt

.SH OPTIONAL DEPENDENCIES
.TP
.B macOS
No extra packages required (\fBpbcopy\fR is built in).
.TP
.B Debian/Ubuntu X11
.B apt install xsel
or
.B apt install xclip
.TP
.B Debian/Ubuntu Wayland
.B apt install wl-clipboard
.TP
.B Fedora X11
.B dnf install xsel
or
.B dnf install xclip
.TP
.B Fedora Wayland
.B dnf install wl-clipboard
.TP
.B Arch Linux
.B pacman -S xsel
or
.B pacman -S wl-clipboard
.TP
.B FreeBSD
.B pkg install xsel
or
.B pkg install xclip

.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.TP
.B "cb: No usable clipboard backend detected"
On a graphical Linux desktop, install \fBxsel\fR, \fBxclip\fR, or
\fBwl-clipboard\fR as appropriate. Over SSH, ensure your terminal supports OSC
52 and try \fBcb -v --show-backend\fR. On macOS, \fBpbcopy\fR should normally
be available without extra packages.
.TP
.B "xsel: Can't open display"
This indicates X11 clipboard tools were invoked without a display. Upgrade to
a current \fBcb\fR version or use \fB--backend=osc52\fR over SSH.
.TP
.B "Wayland session detected, but wl-copy is unavailable"
Install \fBwl-clipboard\fR or use \fB--backend=osc52\fR/\fBxsel\fR if running
XWayland with \fBDISPLAY\fR set.
.TP
.B tmux/screen clipboard not syncing
Enable terminal passthrough in tmux (\fBset -g allow-passthrough on\fR in tmux
3.3+) or configure your terminal's OSC 52 support.

.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \[bu] 2
Bash variables cannot contain NUL bytes; arbitrary binary clipboard data is not
supported.
.IP \[bu]
OSC 52 support varies by terminal and may be disabled for security.
.IP \[bu]
Clipboard success cannot always be verified for OSC 52; failure is detected
when the terminal is clearly unsuitable.
.IP \[bu]
Configuration files are shell-sourced and can execute arbitrary code.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Success.
.TP
1
Failure (empty input, exceeded max size, no backend, or clipboard write failed).

